    "body": "- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n\r\n@echasnovski Sorry if it's just me, but I have found a problem with the algorithm for converting presented in the paper. After `luv2xyz` XYZ is in the range [0,1] and `xyz2rgb` [XYZ is also in the nominal range of [0, 1].](http://www.brucelindbloom.com/Eqn_XYZ_to_RGB.html). But all axios `c/100` without any reason.\r\n\r\n\r\nAlso, I don't like the algorithm change, because it is really confusing when I compare it to the original. I understand that it has been made in accordance with the provided link.\r\n\r\nAnd also, I would be very grateful if you provided where you got the conditions from, [like](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/9e603a31d3fbc7ee61c9b556cd84e97fa2dcff53/lua/mini/base16.lua#L1487).\r\n",
    "closed_at": "2025-01-16T09:34:47Z",
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> After `luv2xyz` XYZ is in the range [0,1] and `xyz2rgb` [XYZ is also in the nominal range of [0, 1].](http://www.brucelindbloom.com/Eqn_XYZ_to_RGB.html). But all axios `c/100` without any reason.\r\n\r\nIt is not. You can check it with something like this:\r\n- Apply the following changes:\r\n    ```diff\r\n    diff --git a/lua/mini/base16.lua b/lua/mini/base16.lua\r\n    index 989212e..0b79978 100644\r\n    --- a/lua/mini/base16.lua\r\n    +++ b/lua/mini/base16.lua\r\n    @@ -1501,6 +1501,7 @@ H.xyz2luv = function(xyz)\r\n       return { l = l, u = u, v = v }\r\n     end\r\n     \r\n    +_G.log = {}\r\n     H.luv2xyz = function(luv)\r\n       if luv.l == 0 then return { x = 0, y = 0, z = 0 } end\r\n     \r\n    @@ -1517,6 +1518,7 @@ H.luv2xyz = function(luv)\r\n       local y = var_y * 100\r\n       local x = -(9 * y * var_u) / ((var_u - 4) * var_v - var_u * var_v)\r\n       local z = (9 * y - 15 * var_v * y - var_v * x) / (3 * var_v)\r\n    +  table.insert(_G.log, { x = x, y = y, z = z })\r\n       return { x = x, y = y, z = z }\r\n     end\r\n     \r\n    ```\r\n- Run the following command `:lua require('mini.base16').mini_palette('#112233', '#ddeeff')`.\r\n- Check the log with `:=log`. Values are in the [0; 100] range.\r\n\r\nMain source of color conversion algorithm is [documented here](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/9e603a31d3fbc7ee61c9b556cd84e97fa2dcff53/lua/mini/base16.lua#L1397) which indeed contains those `100 * ` and ` / 100` steps.\r\n\r\n> And also, I would be very grateful if you provided where you got the conditions from, [like](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/9e603a31d3fbc7ee61c9b556cd84e97fa2dcff53/lua/mini/base16.lua#L1487).\r\n\r\nThere is later a linear combination of `x`, `y`, `z` in the denominator, so they can't be zero all at once. As they are non-negative, `x + y + z == 0` is a more compact way of writing `x == 0 and y == 0 and z == 0` for which the Luv output is known (as it is a `#000000` color). There might have also been some floating point numeric considerations here, but I am not really sure.",
